---
metadata:
  title: "About Annie Elliot | Author of Mr & Mrs Charles Dickens"
  description: "Annie Elliot is the author of Mr & Mrs Charles Dickens, Her Story. She has an MA in Creative Writing and a BA (Hons) in Literary Studies."
  pageType: "author"
---

<section id="author" class="u-container">
	<h1 class="u-page-title">About the Author</h1>
	<div>
		<div class="u-grid main-grid">
			<!--
				The ladder, declared rather than inherited. The portrait's widest
				layout is the single-column tier just under 60rem — the container's
				content box, ~894 CSS px — and above 60rem it is one half of the
				1160px content box less the 5rem grid gap, so 540 CSS px. 1200
				therefore covers 2x at the wide-viewport size and 1800 covers 2x of
				the widest the photo is ever laid out, which is also the cap
				src/reviews.webc declares, so the site has one number.
				Without a width here the image inherited the global ladder, and
				before "auto" came off that list it emitted a 2837px rendition —
				the source's full width — in both WebP and JPEG.
			-->
			<img
				webc:is="eleventy-image"
				src="assets/images/author.jpg"
				alt="Portrait of Annie Elliot"
				width="400, 640, 800, 1200, 1800"
				sizes="(min-width: 60rem) 50vw, 100vw"
			/>

			<div>
				<p>
					Annie Elliot holds an MA in Creative Writing and a BA (Hons) in
					Literary Studies. Her short stories and flash fiction have been
					successful in literary competitions. She has been longlisted for the
					Mslexia Novel Award and has written a booklet to help teachers
					understand dyslexic children’s difficulties. Before retiring, Annie
					worked as a college lecturer, a civil servant, and a newspaper
					reporter. Today, she tours internationally performing as Mrs Dickens
					telling her story, including regular appearances at the Charles
					Dickens Birthplace Museum in Portsmouth.
				</p>

				<p>
					Explaining why she felt compelled to speak up for this woman from the
					past, Annie said, ‘My research revealed the parallels between Mrs
					Dickens’s personal challenges and mine. Catherine had been married for
					twenty-two years when she discovered Charles had begun an affair with
					a teenager. After twenty years, I discovered my husband had been
					unfaithful throughout our marriage so knew how devastating this is. I
					was determined to use this negative experience creatively to give Mrs
					Dickens the voice she was denied in her lifetime. It was a privilege
					to fulfil her dying wish to tell the world that, despite his public
					denials and cruelty at the end of their marriage, Charles had loved
					her once.’
				</p>

				<p>
					At university, her tutor, the late Fay Weldon, described Annie Elliot
					as a natural writer and her work as ‘completely brilliant’. Struck by
					her talent for the acerbic and moving, the author and screenwriter
					Nikesh Shukla, host of the Brown Baby podcast, complimented Annie on
					her ‘comedian’s eye for detail and absurdity’. The naturalist, author
					and broadcaster Stephen Moss described her bitter-sweet style as funny
					and tragic. Mr & Mrs Charles Dickens is Annie Elliot’s debut novel.
				</p>

				<!--
					The brand marks are inline <svg> rather than <img src="…">, unlike the
					retailer logos on the home page: these take their colour from the
					surrounding text (`fill: currentColor`), and an SVG loaded through
					<img> is an isolated document that `currentColor` cannot reach.
					Paths are Simple Icons’ facebook and instagram glyphs, 24×24 viewBox.

					The Facebook entry is deliberately not a link: Annie gave the page’s
					name, ‘Annie Elliot – Author’, and a Facebook page name is not a URL.
					Link it once the facebook.com/… address is known.
				-->
